  "worked_solution": "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\n- The first line is in slope-intercept form, and the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So the slope of the line $y=-5x+3$ is $-5.$\n\n- To find the slope of $3kx-5y=9,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3kx-5y & =9 \\\\ 5y & =3kx-9 \\\\ y & =\\frac{3k}{5}x-\\frac{9}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the line $3kx-5y=9$ is $\\frac{3k}{5}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n(-5)\\cdot (\\frac{3k}{5})=-1\\,\u27f9\\,k=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
